    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Closing costs in Texas typically land in the 2% to 5% range that Freddie Mac cites nationally, and on a buyer's side they are mostly lender fees, the lender's title policy, escrow and recording charges, a survey and prepaid taxes and insurance. Texas has two quirks that work in your favor: there is no transfer tax, and title insurance premiums are set by the state, so they cost the same at every title company.</p>
<p>Here is what each charge is, who usually pays it, and where you have room to save.</p>

<h2>How much are closing costs in Texas?</h2>
<p>Freddie Mac estimates closing costs generally run 2% to 5% of the purchase price. On a $300,000 home, that range is $6,000 to $15,000, which is why it matters to understand what drives your number.</p>
<p>The biggest variables are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your loan type.</strong> FHA, VA and USDA loans each carry an upfront mortgage insurance premium or guarantee fee, though many buyers finance it into the loan.</li>
<li><strong>Discount points.</strong> Paying points to buy down your rate can add thousands at closing.</li>
<li><strong>Prepaids.</strong> Texas property taxes and coastal homeowners insurance premiums are high, so funding your escrow account takes a real bite.</li>
<li><strong>Your closing date.</strong> It affects prepaid interest and how property taxes are prorated.</li>
</ul>
<p>Lenders must send a Loan Estimate within three business days of your application, and you get a Closing Disclosure at least three business days before closing. Compare the two line by line. To get a ballpark before you apply, try our <a href="/tools/closing-cost-calculator/">closing cost calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>Buyer closing costs in Texas, line by line</h2>
<p>Every closing is different, but most Texas buyers with a mortgage will see these categories on their Closing Disclosure.</p>

<h3>Lender charges</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Origination or underwriting fees</strong> charged by the lender to process and approve the loan.</li>
<li><strong>Discount points</strong>, if you choose to buy down your rate.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal fee</strong>, often paid upfront when the appraisal is ordered.</li>
<li><strong>Credit report, flood certification and tax service fees.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Upfront mortgage insurance or guarantee fees</strong> on FHA, VA and USDA loans.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Title and escrow charges</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lender's title policy.</strong> Your lender requires it, and the buyer customarily pays.</li>
<li><strong>Title endorsements</strong> the lender requires, priced under state rules.</li>
<li><strong>Escrow or closing fee</strong> charged by the title company for handling the closing. This is not set by the state.</li>
<li><strong>Survey amendment.</strong> If you want the owner's policy to cover boundary and encroachment issues beyond "shortages in area," you pay an extra premium, which for residential property is 5% of the owner's policy premium.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Government and third-party charges</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recording fees</strong> paid to the county clerk to record your deed and deed of trust.</li>
<li><strong>Survey</strong>, unless the seller provides an existing survey with a T-47 affidavit or T-47.1 declaration that the title company and lender accept.</li>
<li><strong>HOA fees</strong>, such as transfer fees and resale certificate charges, if the home is in an association.</li>
<li><strong>Home inspection</strong>, usually paid outside closing during the option period.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Prepaids and escrow deposits</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prepaid interest</strong> from your closing date to the end of the month.</li>
<li><strong>First year of homeowners insurance</strong>, plus flood insurance if required.</li>
<li><strong>Initial escrow deposit</strong> for future property tax and insurance payments.</li>
</ul>

<h2>No transfer tax: one Texas advantage</h2>
<p>Many states charge a transfer tax based on the sales price, sometimes more than 1%. Texas does not. In 2015, Texas voters approved a constitutional amendment, now Article VIII, Section 29, that bars the Legislature from enacting a transfer tax on transactions that convey fee simple title to real property.</p>
<p>You will still pay county recording fees, but those are flat administrative charges based on the documents, not a percentage of the price. Texas also has no mortgage recording tax.</p>

<h2>Texas title insurance: state-set rates</h2>
<p>Title insurance is one of the largest single line items, and Texas handles it differently from most states. The Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) promulgates title premiums, and every title company and agent must charge the same basic rate for the same policy amount. Shopping title companies will not change the premium, though escrow fees and service quality can differ.</p>
<p>The current rates took effect March 1, 2026, after TDI ordered a 6.2% reduction to basic premiums. You can check them yourself on the <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/title/documents/titlerates2026.pdf">TDI title rate chart</a>. For policies from $100,001 to $1 million, the basic premium is $780 plus 0.494% of the amount above $100,000.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Policy amount</th><th>Owner's policy basic premium (2026)</th><th>Simultaneous lender's policy</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$200,000</td><td>$1,274</td><td>$100</td></tr>
<tr><td>$300,000</td><td>$1,768</td><td>$100</td></tr>
<tr><td>$400,000</td><td>$2,262</td><td>$100</td></tr>
<tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$2,756</td><td>$100</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Under TDI rate rule R-5, when the lender's policy is issued at the same time as the owner's policy and the loan amount does not exceed the owner's policy amount, the lender's policy premium is $100. Required endorsements add to that, so expect your title line to be more than $100 even when the seller pays for the owner's policy.</p>

<h2>Who pays what in a Texas home sale</h2>
<p>The TREC resale contract lets the parties negotiate most costs, but local custom drives the starting point. Here is how it commonly breaks down in the Houston area.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>Usually paid by</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Owner's title policy</td><td>Seller</td><td>Customary around Houston; chosen by checkbox in the contract</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lender's title policy and endorsements</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Required by the lender</td></tr>
<tr><td>Survey amendment to owner's policy</td><td>Negotiable</td><td>Contract lets you choose buyer or seller</td></tr>
<tr><td>New survey</td><td>Negotiable</td><td>Seller may provide an existing survey with a T-47 affidavit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Escrow fee</td><td>Often split</td><td>Varies by title company and contract</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lender fees, appraisal, prepaids</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Can be offset by seller concessions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Real estate commissions</td><td>Negotiable</td><td>Set by your listing and buyer agreements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property taxes</td><td>Prorated</td><td>Seller credits buyer for days the seller owned the home</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>How Texas property tax prorations work</h3>
<p>Texas property taxes are billed in the fall and due by January 31 of the following year, so taxes are paid in arrears. At closing, the seller credits you for the portion of the year they owned the home, and you pay the full bill when it comes due. The TREC contract prorates current-year taxes through the closing date and allows an adjustment later if the actual bill differs.</p>
<p>That credit reduces your cash to close, but do not spend it. It is meant to cover the seller's share of a tax bill you will pay. After you move in, file for the <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">Texas homestead exemption</a> so next year's bill is lower.</p>

<h2>Closing costs vs cash to close</h2>
<p>Closing costs and cash to close are not the same number, and mixing them up is a common budgeting mistake. Your cash to close is your down payment plus closing costs and prepaids, minus credits.</p>
<p>Credits that reduce what you bring include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Earnest money you already deposited with the title company.</li>
<li>The option fee, which the TREC contract credits to the sales price.</li>
<li>The seller's property tax proration.</li>
<li>Seller concessions, lender credits and any down payment assistance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Texas title companies generally require cash to close by wire or cashier's check. Call your escrow officer at a number you already know to confirm wiring instructions, because wire fraud schemes target buyers right before closing.</p>

<h2>Ways to lower your closing costs in Texas</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Compare Loan Estimates from at least three lenders.</strong> Lender fees and rates vary far more than title premiums. Compare the origination charges and the rate together.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiate seller concessions.</strong> Ask the seller to pay part of your closing costs. Each loan program caps seller contributions, so check the limit with your lender.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for a lender credit.</strong> Accepting a slightly higher rate in exchange for a credit cuts cash at closing. It makes sense if you expect to sell or refinance within a few years.</li>
<li><strong>Use an existing survey.</strong> If the seller has a survey and nothing has changed, a T-47 affidavit or T-47.1 declaration can save you the cost of a new one.</li>
<li><strong>Apply for state assistance.</strong> TSAHC and TDHCA programs offer up to 5% of the loan for down payment and closing costs. Our <a href="/blog/first-time-home-buyer-texas/">first-time home buyer Texas guide</a> explains who qualifies.</li>
<li><strong>Shop your insurance.</strong> Homeowners and flood premiums are a big part of prepaids. Get several quotes during your option period.</li>
<li><strong>Pick your closing date carefully.</strong> Closing late in the month reduces prepaid interest, though it does not change your total cost of the loan.</li>
</ol>
[[product:accordion-file-organizer]]

<h2>Reading your Closing Disclosure before you sign</h2>
<p>You must receive the Closing Disclosure at least three business days before closing. Use that time. Check that the loan amount, rate and monthly payment match what you agreed to, that seller credits and concessions appear, and that the title premiums match the TDI rate for your price.</p>
<p>Ask your lender or escrow officer about any new or higher fee. Some charges can only rise within set limits after the Loan Estimate, and a lender may need to issue a corrected disclosure. This article is general information, so confirm specific charges with your lender, title company or a real estate attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Closing costs in Texas usually fall within the 2% to 5% range, and the biggest swing factors are your lender's fees, points and prepaids. Title premiums are fixed by the state and there is no transfer tax, so put your energy into comparing lenders, negotiating seller concessions and checking every line of your Closing Disclosure.</p>
